PortSwigger Web Security Academy — Basic SSRF against the local server

Objective

Exploit a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability to access an internal admin panel and delete the user "carlos".

Environment

My laptop is currently unavailable, so I continued this lab from my phone using:

- Chrome
- Termux
- curl
- PortSwigger Web Security Academy

What is SSRF?

Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) occurs when an application allows an attacker to influence a request made by the server.

This can allow an attacker to make the server communicate with internal resources that the attacker cannot access directly.

Methodology

The application had a product stock-check feature that accepted a "stockApi" parameter.

The normal stock-check functionality used an external stock server.

I tested whether I could control the destination of the server-side request.

I first confirmed that the normal stock check worked through Chrome.

I then used Termux and curl to send a request through the vulnerable stock-check endpoint, changing the "stockApi" value to:

"http://localhost/admin"

The important point was that the request to "localhost" was made by the vulnerable server.

The response returned the internal admin panel, which contained the available users and their administrative actions.

The panel exposed the deletion endpoint:

"/admin/delete?username=carlos"

I then sent the deletion URL through the same vulnerable "stockApi" parameter using curl:

"http://localhost/admin/delete?username=carlos"

The command itself did not display a useful response, so I did not assume the action had succeeded.

Instead, I refreshed the PortSwigger lab in Chrome and verified the actual result.

The lab status changed to:

LAB — Solved ✅

Key Lesson

SSRF is powerful because the attacker does not necessarily need direct access to an internal service.

If the application can be manipulated into making the request, the server's own network access can become the attacker's pathway to internal resources.

Root Cause

The application trusted user-controlled input to determine where the server should make a request without adequately restricting the destination.

Potential Impact

Depending on the application and network environment, SSRF can potentially allow attackers to:

- Access internal administrative interfaces
- Interact with internal APIs
- Read sensitive internal resources
- Reach services that are not publicly accessible
- Access cloud metadata services
- Perform privileged actions through trusted internal services

Main Takeaway

When an application accepts a user-controlled URL, the destination needs to be properly validated and restricted.

A request originating from the server is not automatically trustworthy.

